{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more left-skewed (i.e., where favorable outcomes are relatively more probable compared to unfavorable ones). To evaluate skewness in this context, one must assess the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. Lottery A offers a 0.1% chance of 6000 Euros and 99.9% chance of 0 Euros. Lottery B offers a 0.2% chance of 3000 Euros and 99.8% chance of 0 Euros. Both lotteries have only one nonzero (favorable) outcome and a high probability of zero (unfavorable outcome). Skewness in such binary-outcome lotteries can be interpreted by comparing the probability-weighted asymmetry of outcomes. However, since both lotteries have a single positive outcome and the rest zero, the concept of skewness is limited. Still, Lottery B has a higher probability of a favorable outcome (0.2% vs 0.1%), though the outcome is smaller. Thus, Lottery B could be considered more left-skewed because it assigns a higher probability to a favorable (nonzero) outcome, even if the payoff is lower. Therefore, the decision reason can be applied: it would prefer Lottery B as it has a higher probability of a nonzero (favorable) outcome, making it relatively more left-skewed.",
